<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">P. V. Narasimha Rao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pamulaparthi Venkata Narasimha Rao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">P. V. Narasimha Rao was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> politician who served as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1991 to 1996. Taking office during a severe balance-of-payments crisis, he and his finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a> dismantled the licence system, devalued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> and opened the economy to foreign trade and investment. His government also faced the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in 1992 and the communal violence that followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/p-v-narasimha-rao.jpg" alt="P. V. Narasimha Rao" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Lambiotte - European Commission · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visit_of_Narasimha_Rao,_Indian_Minister_for_Foreign_Affairs,_to_the_CEC_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government of P.V. <b>Narasimha Rao</b>, with Manmohan Singh as finance minister, responded by dismantling the licence system, opening the economy to foreign investment and reducing tariffs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laknepalli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.95,79.83&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Laknepalli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6716 1498 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of P. V. Narasimha Rao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7166 1768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7166 1768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.95,79.83&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
